1
00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:04.471
In this lesson, you'll learn how OCR has evolved over time,

2
00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:08.174
from early approaches relying on the shapes of letters

3
00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:10.477
to modern deep learning systems.

4
00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:14.180
In the lab, you'll use one such deep learning algorithm

5
00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:16.983
to run a detection and recognition pipeline

6
00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:19.786
on documents with real world messiness.

7
00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:23.456
You'll get to see the improvements that deep learning brings,

8
00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:26.760
while bumping into new challenges related to document layouts

9
00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:31.131
and reading order. Okay, let's go. Welcome back, everyone.

10
00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:35.735
In lesson one, we focused on simple document processing systems.

11
00:00:36.236 --> 00:00:40.440
Parsing, OCR, and then adding an agentic brain on top.

12
00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:45.145
In lesson two, four decades of OCR evolution,

13
00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:47.447
we're going to zoom in on OCR itself

14
00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:50.250
and look at how it's evolved over time.

15
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.453
Now, we cannot possibly cover every advance,

16
00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:56.556
every researcher, and every important paper.

17
00:00:57.157 --> 00:00:59.759
So we're going to focus the backstory on

18
00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:03.263
two technologies that are representative of their eras.

19
00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:06.166
Those are Tesseract and PaddleOCR.

20
00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:09.269
Have you heard of them? Have you used them?

21
00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:11.871
They may not be what you think they are

22
00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:14.274
because they too have evolved over time.

23
00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:17.977
Let's start by placing Tesseract and PaddleOCR

24
00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:20.280
into their corresponding eras.

25
00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:23.283
Tesseract stands for the traditional,

26
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.985
procedural computer vision approach.

27
00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:30.590
Lots of hand engineering, lots of steps, lots of rules.

28
00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.694
PaddleOCR is representative of the Deep Learning era.

29
00:01:35.195 --> 00:01:37.397
It uses neural networks end-to-end

30
00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:39.699
for detection and recognition.

31
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.704
And the shift from Tesseract style to PaddleOCR style systems

32
00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.674
really mirrors the broader shift in AI

33
00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:51.678
from carefully designed pipelines with handcrafted features

34
00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:56.516
to data-driven trainable models that learn directly from examples.

35
00:01:57.117 --> 00:02:00.520
If you're interested in the full story, check out the suggested reading.

36
00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:04.524
Now, you already had a chance to use Tesseract in lab one.

37
00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:07.327
But here's a little bit more of the backstory.

38
00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:10.530
And the story matters because this was state-of-the-art

39
00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.932
as recently as 20 years ago.

40
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.902
The rigid sequence of line finding,

41
00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:18.204
word recognition, character classification

42
00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:20.473
is still what comes to mind

43
00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.609
when you say OCR to many people,

44
00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:25.211
especially outside the technology domain.

45
00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:27.914
So, a couple of things to know about Tesseract.

46
00:02:28.615 --> 00:02:32.218
It was proprietary to Hewlett Packard for the 80s and 90s

47
00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.921
and then was released as open source in 2005

48
00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:37.624
and has since been maintained by Google.

49
00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:40.193
In lab one, when you use

50
00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:42.428
Tesseract, you were actually using version 5.

51
00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:47.795
Now Tesseract still performs well on clean printed documents such as books.

52
00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:49.903
But you should think of a

53
00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:51.604
novel that has no pictures in it,

54
00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:54.807
not something like a physics textbook with a bunch of diagrams.

55
00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:59.612
It supports many languages and it can run on resource constrained systems

56
00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.614
because it only needs a CPU.

57
00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:05.418
You already saw some of the limitations in lab one.

58
00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:09.022
Any sort of text-in-the-wild that isn't nice black and white

59
00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:11.724
and straight lines is going to be a problem.

60
00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:16.196
So for the history buffs out there and for you visual learners,

61
00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:18.398
take a look at some of these examples

62
00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:21.901
that were embedded in the 2017 Tesseract paper.

63
00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:27.540
Notice how the figures really demonstrate this hand engineering

64
00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.844
of understanding the shapes and spacing of individual letters.

65
00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:34.147
We're going to move on from Tesseract now,

66
00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:36.749
and it's important to note that

67
00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:39.118
although this was the earliest OCR approach,

68
00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:42.222
you should not think of it as foundational technology.

69
00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.624
What comes next in the next

70
00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:46.826
era is actually an entirely new approach.

71
00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:49.529
So, stated another way, the deep learning

72
00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:51.864
approaches that we're about to look at

73
00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:56.569
do not build on the code or the architectural concepts from Tesseract.

74
00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.172
So, enter the deep learning era.

75
00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.274
Starting around 2015,

76
00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:05.078
the deep learning approach to OCR really becomes the new standard.

77
00:04:05.478 --> 00:04:08.481
And the OCR problem is broken into two stages,

78
00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:11.084
and those can now be optimized separately.

79
00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:14.287
That makes the overall process much more modular.

80
00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:17.223
The first is Text Detection. Find all

81
00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:19.459
of the regions that contain the text.

82
00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.061
And the second is Text Recognition.

83
00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:24.964
Read the content of each text region.

84
00:04:25.732 --> 00:04:28.334
So in today's lab for lesson two,

85
00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:31.838
you're going to use PaddleOCR developed by Baidu.

86
00:04:32.038 --> 00:04:34.841
It's one such deep learning OCR system,

87
00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:37.944
and it's open source with fairly broad adoption.

88
00:04:38.244 --> 00:04:41.948
As of 2025, PaddleOCR is on version 3.

89
00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:45.852
And in version three, the text detection comes from DBNet,

90
00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:49.956
which stands for differential binarization network.

91
00:04:50.156 --> 00:04:53.459
And the text recognition comes from SVTR,

92
00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:57.263
which stands for Short Vision Transformer Recognizer.

93
00:04:57.797 --> 00:05:01.701
Now, a couple notable strengths include its handling of complex,

94
00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:03.903
irregular, and curved text,

95
00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:07.106
high efficiency when accelerated by GPUs,

96
00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:09.709
and several lightweight deployment options.

97
00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:13.046
This is the architecture diagram that accompanies

98
00:05:13.046 --> 00:05:15.948
the 2025 paper called PaddleOCR 3.0

99
00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:20.053
technical report linked at the bottom.

100
00:05:20.053 --> 00:05:22.955
Within this illustration, notice the gray box

101
00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:27.260
for the text detection module ending in _det.

102
00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:32.198
And then the text recognition module ending in _rec.

103
00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:37.070
Surrounding those two, notice the Preprocessing on the left

104
00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.505
and the line orientation in the center.

105
00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:43.576
Now, follow with your eyes the input image on the left.

106
00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:46.479
It kind of looks like a lotion bottle to me.

107
00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.450
First, the whole input is rotated counterclockwise.

108
00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:53.720
And then all the text areas are detected

109
00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.422
and you see some red rectangles overlaid.

110
00:05:57.523 --> 00:06:01.728
One of those rectangles is actually on top of the lotion bottle

111
00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:04.130
and is rotated relative to the other lines,

112
00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:05.832
so that needs to be corrected.

113
00:06:06.733 --> 00:06:10.136
And then finally, the text within the box is recognized

114
00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:13.005
and returned to the user. Compared to

115
00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:15.842
the old pipelines, you can really see

116
00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:18.845
how much of the complexity is now handled by learned models

117
00:06:18.845 --> 00:06:20.546
rather than by fixed rules.

118
00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:24.851
Since this is a course about building document intelligence pipelines,

119
00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:28.221
let's touch on when you should build with each of these tools.

120
00:06:28.721 --> 00:06:30.523
And let's just go row by row.

121
00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:32.925
So, I think we've already covered the

122
00:06:32.925 --> 00:06:35.528
Core Approach and the underlying technology for each.

123
00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:39.232
Good news is the licensing here, they're both Open-source.

124
00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:42.435
As far as best fit use cases,

125
00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:46.139
Tesseract is really going to be ideal for document scanning.

126
00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:48.141
Think really about books.

127
00:06:48.441 --> 00:06:51.411
Anytime that you've got black on white text

128
00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:53.613
and the layout is fairly regular.

129
00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:58.518
PaddleOCR is going to perform better with some real-world use cases.

130
00:06:58.518 --> 00:07:01.354
It's going to be able to handle signage

131
00:07:01.354 --> 00:07:04.223
and receipts and complex layouts in the content.

132
00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:07.927
As far as which particular languages and tools,

133
00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:10.296
both of them support a wide range of tools,

134
00:07:10.296 --> 00:07:13.199
including Latin and non-Latin characters,

135
00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:16.302
and they both have easy integrations with Python.

136
00:07:16.903 --> 00:07:21.507
As far as deployment options, think of Tesseract as fairly lightweight

137
00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:24.811
versus being part of the PaddleOCR framework

138
00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:28.014
is really much more of a complete toolkit.

139
00:07:28.781 --> 00:07:31.851
To wrap up the slide portion of this lesson,

140
00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:35.555
it's important to acknowledge that both Tesseract and PaddleOCR

141
00:07:35.655 --> 00:07:38.958
really stand for an era of OCR development.

142
00:07:39.659 --> 00:07:42.862
There are many other OCR solutions to choose from

143
00:07:42.862 --> 00:07:45.465
and these are simply two among many.

144
00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:47.967
And in lesson four, we're going

145
00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:50.169
to introduce yet another one not listed,

146
00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:52.672
which belongs in the agentic era.

147
00:07:53.172 --> 00:07:55.374
So it's lab time again.

148
00:07:55.575 --> 00:07:58.578
In this lab, you'll get hands-on with PaddleOCR,

149
00:07:58.678 --> 00:08:01.581
and by the end of the lab, you should feel comfortable

150
00:08:01.681 --> 00:08:04.784
with running PaddleOCR on your own images,

151
00:08:04.984 --> 00:08:06.986
interpreting its outputs,

152
00:08:07.086 --> 00:08:09.989
and thinking about how to plug it into larger document processing

153
00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:12.058
or agentic systems.
